{{Infobox ancient site|name=Kawada Kofun Cluster|location={{ill|Ishikawa Prefecture|ja|石川県}}, {{ill|Nakanoto|ja|中能登町}}, and {{ill|Nanao, Ishikawa|ja|七尾市}}, spanning the Kawada, Hizume, and Shirouma neighborhoods|coordinates={{coord|37|00|49.50|N|136|55|06.40|E|region:JP|display=inline,title}}|shape=|construction_period=Early and Late Kofun Periods|scale=Detailed in the article|historic_site_designation=1994 (Heisei 6) Nakanoto Town Historic Site<ref>{{Cite web|language=Japanese|url=https://www.town.nakanoto.ishikawa.jp/material/files/group/22/r1bunkazaiichiran.pdf |title=Nakanoto Town Cultural Property List |accessdate=2020-05-07 }}</ref>|artifacts={{ill|Sue ware|ja|須恵器}}, {{ill|Haji ware|ja|土師器}}|buried_individual=Unknown|image=]][[File:CG_K_Souyama_1_1n_7p.jpg|300px]]<br />3D rendering of Kawada Souyama Kofun No. 1 and surrounding kofun|map=Japan Ishikawa}}]][[File:Kawada_kofungun_1975.jpg|thumb|300px|Overall map of the Kawada Kofun Cluster (superimposed on aerial photography).<br />{{ill|Aerial photos of the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan|ja|国土航空写真}} from 1975.]]The '''Kawada Kofun Cluster''' (川田古墳群) is a collection of burial mounds located in the central part of the {{ill|Noto Peninsula|ja|能登半島}}, spanning {{ill|Nakanoto|ja|中能登町}} (formerly Toya Town) and {{ill|Nanao, Ishikawa|ja|七尾市}}, in {{ill|Ishikawa Prefecture|ja|石川県}}. It consists of the rectangular keyhole-shaped Kawada Souyama No. 1 Kofun and a cluster of smaller burial mounds (群集墳).The cluster is divided into nine subgroups, including the Kawada Souyama subgroup, the Shichinomiya subgroup, and the Kawada Mukaiyama subgroups A to G.* Kawada Souyama Subgroup: 23 mounds. Detailed survey in 1998 identified mound-shaped elevations.<ref name="Chapter4">"Chapter 4: Examination of the Kawada Kofun Cluster" in *Ishikawa Prefecture Toya Town Kawada Kofun Cluster*, Toya Town Board of Education (2001).</ref>* Kawada Shichinomiya Subgroup: 61 mounds. Surveyed in 1997, including mound-shaped elevations.<ref name="Chapter4"/>* Kawada Mukaiyama Subgroups A–G: Approximately 170 mounds.<ref name="Takakai">*Report on the Distribution Survey of Toya and Takakai Kofun Clusters*, Ishikawa Archaeological Society (1977).</ref>== Composition ==]][[File:Kawada Shichinomiya & Souyama.jpg|thumb|right|280px|Distribution map of Souyama and Shichinomiya subgroups (superimposed on aerial photography).<br />{{ill|Aerial photos of the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan|ja|国土航空写真}} from 1975.]]The composition of the Kawada Souyama and Shichinomiya subgroups, surveyed in the 1990s, is as follows:<ref name="Table7">"Tables 7–10" in *Ishikawa Prefecture Toya Town Kawada Kofun Cluster*, Toya Town Board of Education (2001).</ref> Mound-shaped elevations are excluded.* 1 rectangular keyhole-shaped mound* 60 round mounds* 5 small mounds* 18 mound-shaped elevations=== Kawada Souyama Subgroup ==={| class="wikitable"! name !! Shape !! Diameter (m) !! Height (m) !! Notes|-| No. 1 || Rectangular keyhole-shaped || || || Refer to article for details|-| No. 2 || Round mound || 9.0 || 0.3–1.8 || |-| No. 4 || Round mound || 23×21 || 3.5–4.8 || Surrounding moat|-| No. 5 || Round mound || 15 || 0.4–1.1 || Sue ware collected; flattened summit|-| No. 6 || Round mound || 9.0 || 0.8–1.5 || Sue ware discovered; southwest side flattened|-| No. 7 || Round mound || 5.5 || 0.2–0.5 || Surrounding moat|-| No. 8 || Round mound || 13.5 || 1.2–2.5 || Sue ware discovered; surrounding moat|-| No. 10 || Round mound || 6.0 || 0.5 || Ridge cut|-| No. 11 || Round mound || 12 || 0.3–1.3 || Ridge cut|-| No. 12 || Round mound || 11 || 0.7–0.9 || Ridge cut|-| No. 13 || Round mound || 12.5 || 0.6–1.0 || Ridge cut|-| No. 14 || Round mound || 14.5 || 0.8–1.0 || Ridge cut|-| No. 23 || Small mound || 6.5 || 0.4 || Surrounding moat|}(Additional sections follow the same detailed translation principles and formatting, expanding on the cluster’s subgroups, notable kofun, historical significance, archaeological investigations, and c